Title: Economic Organization through Innovation Capital Formation: The Emergence of Coordinated Knowledge Economies
Abstract: Innovation Capital Formation (ICF) addresses the economic architecture through which knowledge and invention can progress toward sustained economic activity. Building upon the financial, institutional, and transitional organization developed in preceding research, this paper examines the broader economic organization that can emerge as shared coordination infrastructure supports economic translation across a larger population of knowledge and invention. It introduces economic configuration as an intermediate analytical level between invention and aggregate economic activity: the arrangement of rights, capabilities, resources, participants, capital, relationships, and market connections through which invention progresses toward and participates in economic activity. Economic configuration does not replace coordination as the underlying organizational requirement; it describes what coordination makes possible around particular knowledge and invention. The paper argues that economic organization has an accumulative and potentially reinforcing character. As economic configurations form, operate, and change, they generate interactions through which technical, professional, institutional, financial, and coordination capabilities can develop. Where those capabilities remain accessible and reusable, prior economic organization can become an input into subsequent processes of innovation. The distinctive contribution of shared coordination infrastructure lies in its potential to make selected interfaces and coordination capabilities reusable across otherwise independent economic configurations, extending organizational capability beyond the environments in which it was initially developed. This identifies a potential transmission mechanism from asset-level economic formation to broader economic organization. ICF does not prescribe which inventions should progress, how capital should be allocated, or which configurations should persist. Rather, it can expand the conditions for decentralized economic evaluation, participation, reconfiguration, and continuity. At sufficient scale, the resulting accumulation of coordination and configurational capability can affect how effectively a knowledge economy connects knowledge and invention with productive capability, capital, institutions, and markets. Economic organization under ICF is therefore concerned not only with how invention becomes economic activity, but with how an economy becomes increasingly capable of organizing that process.
